The problem asks us to simulate the number of stones each alien has after $N$ years.
There are $N$ aliens.
Alien $i$ (1-indexed, let's say $i$ goes from 1 to $N$) starts with $A_i$ stones.
Alien $i$ becomes an adult exactly $i$ years later.
When alien $i$ becomes an adult (at year $i$), every *other* adult who currently has at least 1 stone gives 1 stone to the newly adult alien.
We need to find the number of stones each alien has after $N$ years.

Let $C_i^{(t)}$ be the number of stones alien $i$ has at time $t$.
Initially, at $t=0$, $C_i^{(0)} = A_i$.
For a year $t$ from 1 to $N$:
Alien $t$ becomes an adult.
At this moment (year $t$), the aliens who are adults are $1, 2, \ldots, t$.
Any adult alien $j \in \{1, \ldots, t\}$ with $C_j^{(t)} > 0$ gives 1 stone to alien $t$.
Wait, the problem says "every adult who has at least one stone". The newly adult alien $t$ just became an adult, but does $t$ give stones to $t$? No, $t$ receives stones.
